{"title":"Ishika's Leaf Discovery","gradeSubjectTag":"Class 5 · Science · Plant Leaves","readMinutes":2,"scenes":[{"label":"hook","text":"Ishika was sitting in her grandmother's garden on a warm afternoon, looking at the different plants around her. She noticed the bright green leaves on the neem tree, the curry plant, and even the small herbs in the kitchen garden. She wondered why all these leaves were so green."},{"label":"problem","text":"Her younger brother asked her, 'Why are leaves green and not red or yellow like flowers?' Ishika didn't have a good answer. She decided to find out the real reason."},{"label":"mathMoment","text":"Ishika's grandmother explained that leaves have a special pigment called chlorophyll that makes them green. Chlorophyll helps the leaves catch sunlight and turn it into food and energy for the plant. The green colour you see is because chlorophyll absorbs the red and blue colours of sunlight, but it reflects back the green light, so our eyes see green. That's why leaves are green—it's nature's way of helping plants make their own food."},{"label":"wrapUp","text":"Ishika looked at the leaves again with new eyes. Now she understood that the green colour wasn't just pretty—it was doing an important job. She told her brother the answer, and they both went to help water the plants, knowing now that those green leaves were busy making food from the sun."]}],"visual":null,"equations":[],"tryTogether":{"question":"You have 3 plants in your home, and each plant has 7 leaves that are green because of chlorophyll. How many green leaves are there in total?","answer":"21"},"parentPrompt":"Ask Ishika to look at a green leaf together and describe what she now knows chlorophyll is doing in that leaf."}
